["er.eventlog.import-anchor/1",{"authority":{"logical_scope":"aep","stream_identity":"01a0cae6-7e88-710c-8913-d70d29cb7b4c","tenant":"planning"},"completeness":"available_evidence_only","evidence":[],"instance":{"entity":"aep.entity","fields":{"document":{"events":[],"fields":{"$aep":{"archived":false,"metadata":{"created_at":0,"id":"MIG2f8e34a508716d706353705f41a7842fed6f7a8c8a290bce4b48a21b3923df5a","locator":"ep://planning/store/story/cross-repo-relations","provenance":{"created_by":"system","updated_by":"system"},"revision":4,"type":"aep.story/v1","updated_at":0}},"body":"# Story: A relation whose target lives in another member\n\n## Outcome\n\nA story blocked by work in another repository says so in its own frontmatter, and anybody reading\nthe board sees the edge — instead of discovering it when the blocked work is picked up.\n\n## Context\n\n`blocks`, `depends_on` and `derived_from` already exist as a vocabulary; nothing about the relation\nnames changes. What is new is that the target resolves somewhere else, and that failing to resolve\nit is a normal condition: people check out different subsets of a workspace, and a plan that was\n\"broken\" because a colleague's repository is missing from your disk would be a plan nobody could\nread.\n\n## Acceptance\n\nA relation target may name another member; it resolves through the assembly's index; an\nunresolvable target is a **typed fact** carried in the report, not an error and not a silent drop;\n`protocol workspace crossings` lists every relation whose two ends are in different members, naming\nboth; and cycle detection still holds over the assembled graph, including a cycle that exists only\nonce two members are read together.\n\n## Out of Scope\n\nWriting a relation into another member's store. `relate` still writes locally only.\n\n## Open Questions\n\nNone outstanding.\n","relations":[{"decomposes":"epic:one-cli-many-repositories"},{"depends_on":"story:assemble-across-sources"}],"status":"implemented","summary":"blocks, depends_on and derived_from already exist as a vocabulary; what is new is that the target resolves elsewhere - and that an unresolvable one is a typed fact, not an error, because a member nobody has checked out is a normal condition rather than a broken plan.","title":"A relation whose target lives in another member"},"lifecycle_state":"implemented"}},"id":"MIG2f8e34a508716d706353705f41a7842fed6f7a8c8a290bce4b48a21b3923df5a","lifecycle_state":"recorded","revision":4,"version":1},"order":"per_kind_only","subject":["aep.entity","MIG2f8e34a508716d706353705f41a7842fed6f7a8c8a290bce4b48a21b3923df5a"]}]